+\medskip\noindent\emph{Step 1c(i): conditional genealogy.}\quad
+We work with one coordinate $i$ and age $r$, setting
+$h_{s,i}^{[r]}=0$ for $r>s$. Write $L=k+1$ and let $I$ be an
+interval of $m\leq L$ creation times. For endpoints satisfying
+$s\leq b_s\leq s+L-1$, we will show that
+\begin{align}
+&\E\left|\sum_{s\in I}
+ \{m(\xi_{b_s})(h_{s,i}^{[r]})-\rho_{b_s}(h_{s,i}^{[r]})\}\right|^2
+ \leq \frac{CG'(\theta)^2\kappa^{2r}(r+2)^4mL}{J}.
+\label{eq:review-joint-block-mse}
+\end{align}
+Under (A6), set $a_s=\max(1,s-r-1)$. Under (A6-J), set $a_s=s$,
+use the joint state, and retain only $r=0$ throughout. In either case,
+let $\mathcal C$ be the sigma-field generated by the prediction clouds
+from time one through the largest endpoint plus one.
+If an endpoint is $N$, append a mutation
+to a one-point space after the actual time-$N$ resampling. This records
+the final selected parents without changing the score. The joint
+density, conditional on these clouds, factors over child nodes. For
+$u\geq2$, the conditional probability that child $j$ has parent $l$ is
+\begin{equation}
+B_u^J(j,l)=
+\frac{g_{u-1,l}^{\phi}
+ f_{X_u|X_{u-1}}(X_{u,j}^{P,\phi}\mid X_{u-1,l}^{P,\phi};\phi)}
+{\sum_v g_{u-1,v}^{\phi}
+ f_{X_u|X_{u-1}}(X_{u,j}^{P,\phi}\mid X_{u-1,v}^{P,\phi};\phi)}.
+\label{eq:review-backward-parent}
+\end{equation}
+Consequently, for a fixed $0\leq\varrho_*<1$ depending only on the stated
+bounds,
+\begin{align}
+\max_{j,l}B_u^J(j,l)&\leq C/J,&
+B_u^J(j,l)&\geq(1-\varrho_*)
+ \frac{g_{u-1,l}^{\phi}}{\sum_vg_{u-1,v}^{\phi}},&
+\operatorname{osc}(B_u^J f)&\leq\varrho_*\operatorname{osc}(f).
+\label{eq:review-backward-contraction}
+\end{align}
+We may enlarge $\varrho_*$ to cover the exact forward conditional
+kernels as well. It is a proof constant, distinct from the $\varrho$
+in the population-bias bound.
+\endgraf
+Conditional on a parent--child pair, retain the regular conditional law
+of its simulator draw. These draws and parent choices are independent
+across distinct child nodes. At time one the initial histories are iid
+before conditioning, and remain independent conditional on their
+respective $X_{1,j}^{P,\phi}$. Thus no time-zero resampling is introduced.
+Integrating these conditional simulator laws in $h_{s,i}^{[r]}$ gives a
+bounded function of the physical block $X_{a_s:s}$, with supremum norm
+at most $CG'(\theta)\kappa^r$. For $a_s=1$, the initial history is
+integrated conditional on $X_1$.
+\endgraf
+There is one prediction child at $b_s+1$ for each filtered particle at
+$b_s$. Its inherited $h_{s,i}^{[r]}$ is unchanged. Hence
+$\E[m(\xi_{b_s})(h_{s,i}^{[r]})\mid\mathcal C]$ is the backward
+smoother with uniform terminal weights at $b_s+1$, not the weighted
+smoother at $b_s$. The extra transition has no observation, so its
+population target remains $\rho_{b_s}(h_{s,i}^{[r]})$.
+\endgraf
+\medskip\noindent\emph{Step 1c(ii): error of the conditional mean.}\quad
+We apply the finite-particle telescoping identity (7.1) of
+\citet{delmoral10backward} and give the local ratio calculation to
+track the second moment and weak bias. For a bounded function $f$
+of a block $X_{a:s}$, the operator in that identity uses empirical
+backward kernels before time $u$ and exact transitions after $u$.
+It is measurable before the time-$u$ prediction draws
+(their Definition 5.1). Fix $T=b_s+1$ and let $\mathcal H_{u-1}$
+contain the prediction clouds through $u-1$, with $\mathcal H_0$
+trivial. Let $\mu_u$ be the conditional prediction law given
+$\mathcal H_{u-1}$ and $\eta_u^J$ its empirical measure. At $u=1$,
+$\mu_1$ is the common law of the iid first predictions.
+In the notation of that identity, put
+\[
+q_u=\frac{Q_{u,T}1}{\mu_u(Q_{u,T}1)},\qquad
+F_u=\frac{D_{u,T}^Jf}{Q_{u,T}1}.
+\]
+Here $Q_{u,T}$ includes the current measurement potential at $u$
+and all potentials through $T-1$, while the terminal potential at $T$
+is one. The operator $D_{u,T}^J$ additionally integrates the empirical
+backward history before $u$. Both functions are
+$\mathcal H_{u-1}$-measurable. The local ratio difference is exactly
+\begin{align}
+d_u&=q_u\{F_u-\mu_u(q_uF_u)\},&
+\mu_uq_u&=1,&\mu_ud_u&=0,\notag\\
+e_u&=\frac{(\eta_u^J-\mu_u)d_u}{\eta_u^Jq_u}
+ =\frac{\eta_u^J(q_uF_u)}{\eta_u^Jq_u}-\mu_u(q_uF_u)\notag\\
+&\quad
+ =(\eta_u^J-\mu_u)d_u
+ -\frac{\{(\eta_u^J-\mu_u)d_u\}
+         \{(\eta_u^J-\mu_u)q_u\}}{\eta_u^Jq_u}.
+\label{eq:review-predictable-ratio}
+\end{align}
+The process and measurement bounds give $C^{-1}\leq q_u\leq C$. The forward and
+backward contractions in the proof of their Theorem 3.2 give
+\begin{equation}
+\operatorname{osc}(F_u)\leq C\operatorname{osc}(f)
+\begin{cases}
+\varrho_*^{a-u},&u<a,\\
+1,&a\leq u\leq s,\\
+\varrho_*^{u-s},&u>s.
+\end{cases}
+\label{eq:review-block-oscillation}
+\end{equation}
+Indeed, before $a$ the forward conditional kernels contract up to $a$,
+and after $s$ the empirical backward kernels contract down to $s$.
+Within the block no contraction is required. Conditional iid sampling,
+the lower bound on $q_u$, and conditional Cauchy--Schwarz now give
+\begin{align}
+\{\E(e_u^2\mid\mathcal H_{u-1})\}^{1/2}
+&\leq C\operatorname{osc}(F_u)/\sqrt J,\notag\\
+|\E(e_u\mid\mathcal H_{u-1})|
+&\leq C\sqrt{\frac{\operatorname{osc}(d_u)^2}{J}
+                  \frac{\operatorname{osc}(q_u)^2}{J}}
+ \leq C\operatorname{osc}(F_u)/J.
+\end{align}
+For $f$ obtained by integrating the simulator draws in $h_{s,i}^{[r]}$,
+the telescoping identity reads
+\begin{equation}
+\E[m(\xi_{b_s})(h_{s,i}^{[r]})\mid\mathcal C]
+ -\rho_{b_s}(h_{s,i}^{[r]})=\sum_{u=1}^{T}e_u.
+\end{equation}
+We insert the deterministic bound in
+Equation~(\ref{eq:review-block-oscillation}) before taking
+unconditional norms. Since
+\begin{equation}
+\sum_{u=1}^{T}\varrho_*^{\operatorname{dist}(u,[a_s,s])}
+\leq s-a_s+1+\frac{2}{1-\varrho_*}\leq C(r+2),
+\end{equation}
+Minkowski's inequality and the triangle inequality for expectations
+give, uniformly in $b_s\geq s$,
+\begin{align}
+\|\E[m(\xi_{b_s})(h_{s,i}^{[r]})\mid\mathcal C]
+       -\rho_{b_s}(h_{s,i}^{[r]})\|_{L^2}
+ &\leq CG'(\theta)\kappa^r(r+2)/\sqrt J,\notag\\
+|\E m(\xi_{b_s})(h_{s,i}^{[r]})-\rho_{b_s}(h_{s,i}^{[r]})|
+ &\leq CG'(\theta)\kappa^r(r+2)/J.
+\label{eq:review-uniform-historical-bias}
+\end{align}
+In particular, the squared error of the conditional mean of the sum
+in Equation~(\ref{eq:review-joint-block-mse}) is at most
+$CG'(\theta)^2\kappa^{2r}(r+2)^2m^2/J$.
+\endgraf
+\medskip\noindent\emph{Step 1c(iii): variance conditional on the clouds.}\quad
+We now condition on $\mathcal C$ and reveal the parent choices and
+simulator draws backwards, one time layer at a time. For the endpoint $b_s+1$,
+let $W_{s,u}(j)$ be the fraction of its $J$ labels at time-$u$ node $j$.
+Initially $W_{s,b_s+1}(j)=1/J$. Given the later layers, the choices at
+distinct child nodes are independent. Writing $\mathcal R_u$ for
+$\mathcal C$ and the already revealed layers strictly after $u$,
+\begin{align}
+\E\left[\sum_jW_{s,u-1}(j)^2\mid\mathcal R_u\right]
+&=\sum_jW_{s,u}(j)^2
+ +\sum_{j\ne l}W_{s,u}(j)W_{s,u}(l)
+                   \sum_vB_u^J(j,v)B_u^J(l,v)\notag\\
+&\leq\sum_jW_{s,u}(j)^2+C/J,\notag\\
+\E\left[\sum_jW_{s,u}(j)^2\mid\mathcal C\right]
+&\leq\min\{1,C(b_s-u+2)/J\}.
+\label{eq:review-descendant-collisions}
+\end{align}
+At node $j$ in layer $u$, the conditional average of $h_{s,i}^{[r]}$
+has oscillation at most $CG'(\theta)\kappa^rW_{s,u}(j)c_{s,r}(u)$, where
+\begin{equation}
+c_{s,r}(u)=\mathbf1\{a_s<u\leq s+1\}
+ +\varrho_*^{u-s-1}\mathbf1\{s+1<u\leq b_s+1\},\qquad u\geq2.
+\end{equation}
+The first term bounds choices inside the function's block. The second
+follows by applying Equation~(\ref{eq:review-backward-contraction})
+at each intervening time. Once that block is revealed, earlier choices
+cannot change the function. Set $c_{s,r}(1)=\mathbf1\{a_s=1\}$.
+Layer one reveals the independent initial-history draws, using
+$W_{s,1}$ and this coefficient. Thus $\mathcal R_1$ contains all
+parent layers, while $\mathcal R_0$ additionally contains those draws.
+\endgraf
+Let $Z=\sum_{s\in I}m(\xi_{b_s})(h_{s,i}^{[r]})$. Its conditional
+Doob differences are
+$D_u=\E[Z\mid\mathcal R_{u-1}]-\E[Z\mid\mathcal R_u]$.
+The newly revealed nodes are independent and enter these conditional
+expectations additively. Summing their conditional variances, then
+using the triangle inequality over nodes and
+Equation~(\ref{eq:review-descendant-collisions}), gives
+\begin{align}
+\E[D_u^2\mid\mathcal C]
+&\leq CG'(\theta)^2\kappa^{2r}
+ \E\left[\sum_j\left\{\sum_{s\in I}
+ c_{s,r}(u)W_{s,u}(j)\right\}^2\middle|\mathcal C\right]\notag\\
+&\leq CG'(\theta)^2\kappa^{2r}
+ \left\{\sum_{s\in I}c_{s,r}(u)
+             \sqrt{(b_s-u+2)/J}\right\}^2.
+\end{align}
+Terms before their endpoint is reached are zero. Since $I$ has length
+$m\leq L$,
+\begin{align}
+b_s-u+2&\leq C(L+r+2)\quad\text{on the nonzero terms},\notag\\
+\sup_u\sum_{s\in I}c_{s,r}(u)&\leq C(r+2),&
+\sum_u\sum_{s\in I}c_{s,r}(u)&\leq Cm(r+2).
+\end{align}
+Conditional martingale orthogonality therefore yields
+\begin{align}
+\E\Var(Z\mid\mathcal C)
+&=\sum_u\E D_u^2\notag\\
+&\leq \frac{CG'(\theta)^2\kappa^{2r}}{J}
+ (L+r+2)\sup_u\sum_{s\in I}c_{s,r}(u)
+                   \sum_u\sum_{s\in I}c_{s,r}(u)\notag\\
+&\leq CG'(\theta)^2\kappa^{2r}(r+2)^4mL/J.
+\end{align}
+The conditional mean--variance identity therefore gives
+\begin{align}
+\E\left|Z-\sum_{s\in I}\rho_{b_s}(h_{s,i}^{[r]})\right|^2
+&=\E\Var(Z\mid\mathcal C)
+ +\E\left|\E[Z\mid\mathcal C]
+          -\sum_{s\in I}\rho_{b_s}(h_{s,i}^{[r]})\right|^2\notag\\
+&\leq\frac{CG'(\theta)^2\kappa^{2r}(r+2)^4}{J}(mL+m^2)
+ \leq\frac{CG'(\theta)^2\kappa^{2r}(r+2)^4mL}{J},
+\end{align}
+where we used $m\leq L$ and enlarged $C$ in the last inequality.
+This proves Equation~(\ref{eq:review-joint-block-mse}). Since
+$\sum_{r\geq0}\kappa^r(r+2)^2<\infty$, Minkowski's inequality
+allows us to sum the square roots of these bounds over ages.
+For $I=\{N-k,\ldots,N\}$ and every $b_s=N$, we obtain
+\begin{equation}
+\E\left|m(\xi_N)\left(\sum_{s=N-k}^Nh_{s,i}\right)
+ -\rho_N\left(\sum_{s=N-k}^Nh_{s,i}\right)\right|^2
+ \leq CG'(\theta)^2(k+1)^2/J.
+\label{eq:review-terminal-mse}
+\end{equation}
+\endgraf
+\medskip\noindent\emph{Step 1d: covariance summation.}\quad
+We partition the $N-L$ interior creation times into consecutive groups
+$I_b$ of size $L$, except possibly the last, and write $m_b=|I_b|$ and
+$Z_{b,r}=\sum_{s\in I_b}m(\xi_{s+k})(h_{s,i}^{[r]})$.
+Equation~(\ref{eq:review-joint-block-mse}) bounds their variances by
+$CG'(\theta)^2\kappa^{2r}(r+2)^4m_bL/J$.
+Each group depends on at most $2L+r+2$ consecutive cloud transitions.
+We use the mixing bound $a(v)$ from
+Equation~(\ref{eq:dmop-zero-centered-l4-and-mixing}) and Davydov's inequality.
+The centered $L^4$ norm is at most $CG'(\theta)\kappa^rm_b$.
+Since $m_bm_{b+d}\leq L\sqrt{m_bm_{b+d}}$, we obtain
+\begin{align}
+|\Cov(Z_{b,r},Z_{b+d,r})|
+&\leq CG'(\theta)^2\kappa^{2r}L\sqrt{m_bm_{b+d}}\notag\\[-2pt]
+&\quad\times\min\left\{\frac{(r+2)^4}{J},
+ a((dL-2L-r-3)_+)^{1/2}\right\}.
+\end{align}
+For overlapping windows use the bound one for the mixing term.
+Choose $v_0\leq C(1+\log J)^2$ with $a(v_0)^{1/2}\leq J^{-1}$.
+There are at most $C\{1+(r+v_0)/L\}$ separations before this cutoff.
+The remaining geometric series, sampled every $L$ times, is at most
+$C\{1+(1+\log J)/L\}/J$. Also, for each $d$,
+\begin{equation}
+\sum_b\sqrt{m_bm_{b+d}}
+ \leq\tfrac12\sum_b(m_b+m_{b+d})\leq N-L.
+\end{equation}
+Including the diagonal in the covariance sum consequently gives
+\begin{equation}
+\Var\left(\sum_bZ_{b,r}\right)
+ \leq\frac{CG'(\theta)^2\kappa^{2r}(r+2)^5(N-L)}{J}
+       \{L+(1+\log J)^2\}.
+\end{equation}
+Minkowski over ages, Equation~(\ref{eq:review-terminal-mse}), and
+$\Var(A+B)\leq2\Var(A)+2\Var(B)$ now give
+\begin{align}
+\Var\{\nabla_\theta\hat s_k^1(\theta)\}
+&\lesssim\frac{pG'(\theta)^2}{J}
+ \{N(k+1)+(N-k-1)(1+\log J)^2\},\notag\\
+\|\E\nabla_\theta\hat s_k^1(\theta)-\nabla_\theta s_k^1(\theta)\|_2
+&\lesssim N\sqrt p\,G'(\theta)/J.
+\label{eq:review-truncated-score-bounds}
+\end{align}
+The second inequality follows by summing
+Equation~(\ref{eq:review-uniform-historical-bias}) over ages and the
+$N$ creation times.
+\endgraf
+\medskip\noindent\emph{Step 2: geometric mixture.}\quad
+The mixture weights $\beta_k$ below satisfy
+$\sum_k\beta_k=1$ and
+$\sum_k\beta_k(k+1)=\sum_{r=0}^{N-1}\alpha^r$.
+Centered Minkowski followed by Jensen gives
+\begin{align}
+\Var\{\nabla_\theta\hat\ell^\alpha(\theta)\}
+&\leq\left(\sum_k\beta_k
+       \sqrt{\Var\{\nabla_\theta\hat s_k^1(\theta)\}}\right)^2
+ \leq\sum_k\beta_k\Var\{\nabla_\theta\hat s_k^1(\theta)\}\notag\\
+&\lesssim\frac{pG'(\theta)^2}{J}
+ \left\{N\sum_{r=0}^{N-1}\alpha^r
+ +\left(N-\sum_{r=0}^{N-1}\alpha^r\right)(1+\log J)^2\right\}.
+\label{eq:review-alpha-variance}
+\end{align}
+The mixture of the bias bounds is still $N\sqrt p\,G'(\theta)/J$.
+Thus the proposed particle-MSE replacement for Lemma~
+\ref{lem:dmop-alpha-variance} is
+\begin{align}
+&\E\|\nabla_\theta\hat\ell^\alpha(\theta)
+          -\nabla_\theta\ell^\alpha(\theta)\|_2^2\notag\\
+&\quad\lesssim pG'(\theta)^2\left[
+ \frac{N\sum_{r=0}^{N-1}\alpha^r
+ +(N-\sum_{r=0}^{N-1}\alpha^r)(1+\log J)^2}{J}
+ +\frac{N^2}{J^2}\right].
+\label{eq:review-alpha-particle-mse}
+\end{align}
+At $\alpha=1$, Equation~(\ref{eq:review-terminal-mse}) with $k=N-1$
+directly gives $CpG'(\theta)^2N^2/J$. The bounds hold for every
+$J\geq2$, with constants independent of $N,J$, and $\alpha$.}
